<div class="prose max-w-prose">

As part of my work at Buzuci, I was part of the wedding department, Hennak, and was
involved in one of the most rewarding collaborative projects of the year: 
creating wedding stationery for an editorial-style photoshoot that was later
published in Wedding Guide South Africa.

This project required careful balance. We needed to create a concept that felt
luxurious and elevated, while working within a constrained budget and aligning
with the overall look and direction of the shoot. The stationery had to support
the atmosphere of the photography, while also clearly showcasing the products
and range offered by the business.

## The challenge

The biggest challenge was creating a premium-feeling result without overspending.
The styling had to feel intentional, polished, and cohesive, and it needed to
work within a wider visual story that included florals, venue styling, and the
overall editorial mood. Every element needed to feel considered, from the materials and finishes to the colour palette, typography, and layout.

At the same time, the stationery had to help present new product ideas that the
team expected to be on-trend for the upcoming year. This meant the design had to
balance commercial thinking with creative execution — presenting products in a
way that felt aspirational while still being realistic and commercially viable.

## The collaboration

I worked closely with two other team members to develop the stationery concept
and bring it to life. It was a collaborative process that involved aligning the
creative direction with the shoot’s overall visual theme, coordinating the
materials and details, and ensuring the final pieces complemented the broader
styling rather than competing with it.

That meant thinking beyond individual items and shaping a cohesive design
system: invitations, stationery details, and supporting pieces had to feel part
of one unified story.

## The outcome

The result was a premium, editorial wedding stationery suite that reflected the
luxury mood of the photoshoot while still remaining practical and cost-aware.
The work was published in Wedding Guide South Africa, giving the collection
exposure in a highly relevant market and helping to showcase Buzuci’s Wedding Department, Hennak, product
offerings in a way that felt both aspirational and commercially strong.

This project highlighted the importance of thoughtful design under constraints:
when the budget is limited, the answer is not to reduce the concept, but to
refine it. Strong composition, considered materials, and a clear creative
direction can still create a luxurious impression without unnecessary spend.

